# Real proof search: quantifiers, branching, and two logics

Sources: [leanHaT](https://leancop.de/leanhat/) and
[nanoCoP-i-HT](https://www.leancop.de/ihat/).

Set `SWI_ELIXIR_PATH` to this checkout's absolute path before starting Livebook.

```elixir
project = System.fetch_env!("SWI_ELIXIR_PATH")
Mix.install([{:swi_elixir, path: project}])
Code.require_file(Path.join(project, "examples/showcase.exs"))
prover_sources = [
  {"leanhat.pl", """
  :- module(leanhat, [prove_text/1]).
  :- op(700, xfx, =).
  :- include('#{Path.join(project, "vendor/leanHaT/leanhat_swi.pl")}').
  :- include('#{Path.join(project, "vendor/leanHaT/nanocop_tptp2.pl")}').
  prove_text(Text) :-
      swi_bridge:read_goal(Text, leanhat, Formula, _),
      leancop_equal(Formula, Problem), prove(Problem).
  """},
  {"nanocop.pl", """
  :- module(nanocop, [prove_text/2, prove_text/3]).
  :- op(700, xfx, =).
  :- current_prolog_flag(occurs_check, Flag), nb_setval(nanocop_load_flag, Flag).
  :- include('#{Path.join(project, "vendor/nanoCoP-i-HT/nanocopi20_swi.pl")}').
  :- nb_getval(nanocop_load_flag, Flag), set_prolog_flag(occurs_check, Flag),
     nb_delete(nanocop_load_flag).
  :- include('#{Path.join(project, "vendor/nanoCoP-i-HT/nanocop_tptp2.pl")}').
  :- thread_local lit/4, pathlim/0.
  prove_text(Text, Logic) :- prove_text(Text, Logic, _).
  prove_text(Text, Logic, Proof) :-
      memberchk(Logic, [intu, ht]),
      setup_call_cleanup(
        (current_prolog_flag(occurs_check, Previous), set_prolog_flag(occurs_check, true)),
        (swi_bridge:read_goal(Text, nanocop, Formula, _),
         leancop_equal(Formula, Equal), leancop_intermed(Equal, Logic, Problem),
         prove(Problem, Proof)),
        set_prolog_flag(occurs_check, Previous)).
  """},
  {"prover_showcase.pl", """
  :- module(prover_showcase, [run/7, source/3]).
  :- use_module(library(prolog_wrap)).
  :- use_module(leanhat).
  :- use_module(nanocop).
  core(leanhat, leanhat:prove(_,_,_,_), leanhat:prove/4).
  core(nanocop, nanocop:prove(_,_,_,_,_,_,_,_,_,_), nanocop:prove/10).
  source(Prover, File, Line) :-
      core(Prover, Head, _), clause(Head, _, Ref),
      clause_property(Ref, file(File)), clause_property(Ref, line_count(Line)), !.
  run(Prover, Logic, Text, Result, Calls, Inferences, Proof) :-
      core(Prover, Head, PI),
      setup_call_cleanup(
        (nb_setval(showcase_calls, 0),
         wrap_predicate(Head, showcase, Wrapped,
           (prover_showcase:count, call(Wrapped)))),
        (statistics(inferences, Before),
         (once(search(Prover, Logic, Text, Proof)) -> Result = proved
         ; Result = exhausted, Proof = none),
         statistics(inferences, After), Inferences is After-Before,
         nb_getval(showcase_calls, Calls)),
        (unwrap_predicate(PI, showcase), nb_delete(showcase_calls))).
  count :-
      (nb_current(showcase_calls, N) -> N1 is N+1, nb_setval(showcase_calls, N1) ; true).
  search(leanhat, ht, Text, unavailable) :- leanhat:prove_text(Text).
  search(nanocop, Logic, Text, Proof) :- nanocop:prove_text(Text, Logic, Proof).
  """}
]
load_prolog = fn session ->
  dir = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "prover-showcase-#{:erlang.phash2(prover_sources)}")
  File.mkdir_p!(dir)
  try do
    Enum.each(prover_sources, fn {name, source} ->
      path = Path.join(dir, name)
      File.write!(path, source)
      :ok = SWI.load(session, path)
    end)
  after
    File.rm_rf!(dir)
  end
end
```

## Inspect the actual loaded kernels

`source/3` asks SWI for the file and line of a real recursive proof-search
clause: leanHaT `prove/4`, nanoCoP `prove/10`. It does not report the adapter's
filename. Each cell owns and releases its sessions, including on assertion
failure, so reevaluation does not accumulate engines.

```elixir
{:ok, session} = SWI.start_link(backend: :port)
try do
  :ok = load_prolog.(session)
  for {prover, file} <- [{:leanhat, "leanhat_swi.pl"}, {:nanocop, "nanocopi20_swi.pl"}] do
    [row] = SWI.query(session, "prover_showcase:source(#{prover},File,Line)") |> Enum.to_list()
    {:atom, source} = row["File"]
    true = Path.basename(source) == file
    {prover, Path.basename(source), row["Line"]}
  end
after
  SWI.stop(session)
end
```

## Run and check the workloads

The 20-step chain needs repeated universal instantiation to reach `p(f²⁰(a))`.
The pigeonhole formula has four disjunctive placement assumptions and eighteen
possible collisions. Both have constructive proofs. The four-step chain runs
in all modes; the longer chain runs in intuitionistic nanoCoP mode. The larger
cases can be expensive for HT search, so we do not require every prover to
finish every case.

Prelinearity `(p→q)∨(q→p)` and the quantified witness
`∃y∀x(p(y)→p(x))` are HT theorems, but intuitionistically invalid.
Excluded middle and double-negation elimination are negative controls in both
logics. Every result is checked against the case's expected outcome, rather
than merely checking that the backends agree.

The project diagnostic installs a temporary SWI predicate wrapper around the
recursive kernel. **Calls** counts real kernel entries, including failed
branches; **Inferences** covers parsing, preprocessing, instrumentation and
search. These are execution evidence, not an independent proof checker or an
uninstrumented performance measurement. Runs are serial because predicate
wrappers are shared within an embedded SWI runtime; do not run this diagnostic
concurrently with other prover sessions. Cleanup removes each wrapper.

```elixir
cases = ProverShowcase.cases()
results = for backend <- [:port, :nif] do
  {:ok, session} = SWI.start_link(backend: backend)
  try do
    :ok = load_prolog.(session)
    for example <- cases, {prover, logic} <- example.modes do
      row = ProverShowcase.run(session, prover, logic, example.formula)
      expected = Map.fetch!(example.expected, logic)
      {:atom, ^expected} = row["Result"]
      if prover == :nanocop and expected == "proved" do
        [_ | _] = row["Proof"]
      end
      {backend, example.name, example.source, prover, logic, row["Result"], row["Calls"], row["Inferences"]}
    end
  after
    SWI.stop(session)
  end
end
[port, nif] = Enum.map(results, fn rows ->
  Enum.map(rows, fn {_, name, source, prover, logic, result, _, _} ->
    {name, source, prover, logic, result}
  end)
end)
true = port == nif
List.flatten(results)
```

`proved` means the invoked prover found a proof. `exhausted` means that
invocation failed after its search; these particular negative cases are known
invalid independently. nanoCoP's HT embedding is sound but incomplete, so
failure is not a general invalidity test. A timeout raises `SWI.Error` and is
**unknown**, never a negative verdict. No timeout is accepted as a passing
result in the table above.

## Retrieve a genuine nanoCoP proof

This is nanoCoP's connection proof term, transported by the usual term
encoding. In HT mode nanoCoP proves the intuitionistic translation, so its proof
would concern the transformed problem. Here we display an intuitionistic
function-chain proof directly. We do not reconstruct or independently verify
it. leanHaT returns success only: its `prove/2` argument is a search bound,
not a proof certificate.

```elixir
{:ok, session} = SWI.start_link(backend: :nif)
try do
  :ok = load_prolog.(session)
  row = ProverShowcase.run(session, :nanocop, :intu, ProverShowcase.chain(4))
  [_ | _] = row["Proof"]
  row["Proof"]
after
  SWI.stop(session)
end
```
